  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is <b>The health check only evaluates instance-level status and does not monitor the critical application process.</b> In AWS, the default EC2 or Auto Scaling Group health check primarily monitors the underlying EC2 instance's status (such as system and instance reachability) and does not delve into application-specific processes. If you need the health check to validate whether a particular application process is running and functioning correctly, you must set up either an <b>ELB health check</b> targeting your application's endpoint or utilize a <b>custom health check</b> with scripts. </p><p>Option <i>The time interval configured for the health check is not appropriate</i> refers to how frequently health checks are performed, but it does not address the core issue—that the check isn't monitoring the application process. Option <i>Health checks cannot be set up to monitor application-specific processes</i> is incorrect because AWS allows custom health checks through scripts or ELB, which can target application-level metrics. Option <i>The health check has been set up in an incorrect AWS region</i> isn't relevant here, as an incorrect region would typically prevent any health check results from updating, not cause a false healthy status for the instances.</p><p><b>Tip:</b> Always ensure your health check matches the layer of health you want to monitor—use instance checks for infrastructure and custom or ELB health checks for applications.</p>",
  "query": "<p>If an Auto Scaling Group's health checks are reporting all instances as healthy, but an application relying on a vital process is malfunctioning, what might be the underlying issue with the health check configuration?</p>",
